[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-33", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 33 — the stream replayed whole: INS4 forums and INS5 memberships served; 2333 / 4546 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-33.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 33 - the stream replayed whole: INS4 forums and INS5 memberships served\n\n> Seed: iteration 32's S23. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung SF1\n> subset; SUT levi, driver judah over the LAN; impl :datalog.\n\n## Generate\n\n  Half the update stream (INS5 memberships 48%, INS4 forums 0.6%) was\n  skipped in iteration 32 because the SUT did not serve those types. Now:\n  INS4 asserts forum/title, forum/creationDate, forum/moderator (tags\n  dropped -- no served read uses them, said in the receipt); INS5 reifies\n  the membership as mb:<forum>:<person> with its join date, the shape\n  knows and like already take. The driver parses types 4 and 5 and sends\n  every event; :updates-skipped is 0.\n\n  Verification, by class:\n    - INS4: no post may have landed in the forum yet, so its covering\n      read is IS6 on a post that does -- the SUT answers INS6 with\n      :forum-known (forum held AND moderator has a name) and the driver\n      reads IS6 on that post, expecting the forum id.\n    - INS5: no served read covers memberships (IC05 would). Counted as\n      :no-covering-read -- a third class beside verified and\n      unverifiable, never as visible.\n\n  Control before measuring (curl on levi): INS4 3 datoms, INS5 3 datoms,\n  INS6 into the new forum :forum-known true, IS6 on that post returns\n  [forum \"Album X\" moderator Brian Kelly]; :ins9 is refused with 400.\n\n  Prediction, written first: 8 slots at reads 1536 + TCR 0.0002 keeps\n  passing; at 32 slots the passing read rate drops (2560 -> ~2048)\n  because the events that were skipped now cost write ops; total ops/s\n  rises because they now count.\n\n## Rank\n\n    slots  reads/s  tcr      upd/s  on-time%  thru/s   verdict\n      8     1536    0.0002   1092     58.50    -       FAILS (delay p95 2944) - refutes prediction\n      8     1280    0.0002   1092    100.00   2332.6   pass\n     32     3072    0.00015  1475    100.00   4545.5   pass\n     32     3584    0.00015  1475     42.09    -       FAILS (delay p95 2272) - control\n\n  Read-after-write: 276 checked / 0 not visible / 0-15 outside a full\n  IC07 window / 75-84 unverifiable / 57 posts also read back through IS6\n  (INS4 verified) / 60 INS5 with no covering read. SUT resident (heap\n  781 MB after gc, rss 2.8 GB, swap 24%), 175,847 inserts applied across\n  the sweeps.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Half the prediction was wrong in the other direction: the 32-slot\n    read rate ROSE (2560 -> 3072). Iteration 32's sweep listed 2048,2560\n    and stopped at its last rate without failing -- that row was\n    sweep-limited, not SUT-limited. A sweep that never reaches a failing\n    rate has not measured a ceiling; the iteration-32 row is corrected\n    in the scoreboard note, not deleted (the value was measured; the\n    claim \"highest passing rate\" was not).\n  - At 8 slots the prediction failed the honest way: doubling the\n    stream's sent events (595 -> 1092/s) took the read ceiling from 1536\n    to 1280 -- write ops hold slots like any other op.\n  - M2 now stands at 8/8 inserts with the stream replayed in simulation\n    time under the dependency rule. Gaps to \"updates\": IC05 (so INS5 has\n    a covering read), the ACID suite, and the remaining IC operations.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH (stream coverage 6/8 -> 8/8\ntypes with 0 skipped; 32-slot column 3372 -> 4546, 8-slot 2130 -> 2333;\nboth controls fail). Ratio to the audited No.1: 60 at 8 slots, 31 at 32.\n\nSeeds for iteration 34, in order:\n\n  - Sweeps must end on a failing rate, or say \"not a ceiling\" in the row.\n  - S24 (IC05 new groups): the read that covers INS5.\n  - On decision: ADR-2609121100 (singleton counterparts), then SF1\n    proper on levi with the whole mix under the whole stream.\n  - S15: static tables for the remaining IC operations.\n"}]
